WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:05.910
In Britain, the reaction to the Panama Papers
leak has been a bit weird.

00:00:05.910 --> 00:00:08.170
Politicians have been releasing summaries
of their tax returns,

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:12.800
all falling over each other to try and prove
that they haven't got any offshore accounts

00:00:12.849 --> 00:00:16.200
or arguing strongly that they shouldn't have
to prove

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.240
that they don't have any offshore accounts.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.700
One of them said that requiring politicians
to have public tax returns

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:27.200
was "reminiscent of the Stasi or the KGB".

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.720
Welcome to Norway. Behind me is the Oslo tax
office.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:35.920
And if you want to know what a politician
earns here, you can just ask them.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:37.420
It's not just politicians, though.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:43.510
It's everyone. Tax returns in Norway, Sweden
and Finland have been public for years:

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.899
the countries differ on how much detail they
provide,

00:00:45.899 --> 00:00:48.350
and whether you need to have a good reason
for asking --

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:52.379
but fundamentally the amount you earn is public
knowledge here.

00:00:52.379 --> 00:00:56.399
Now, at this point, we could do the news report
thing,

00:00:56.399 --> 00:00:59.409
and cut to interviews with Norwegian people
on the street,

00:00:59.409 --> 00:01:01.089
asking how they feel about this.

00:01:01.089 --> 00:01:03.260
Or: we could science it,

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:08.009
and look at a paper by economist Ricardo Perez-Truglia
of Microsoft Research.

00:01:08.009 --> 00:01:12.990
That paper combines two things: first, the
Norwegian Monitor Survey,

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:16.960
which, every two years, surveys a representative
sample of Norwegians

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:20.479
and includes questions about happiness and
life satisfaction.

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:23.549
And second, when finding out someone else's
tax details went from

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:26.469
"you have to go here and look through a book,

00:01:26.469 --> 00:01:28.899
"and you've only got three weeks in the year
to do it"

00:01:28.899 --> 00:01:34.140
to "a newspaper has published everything online
and there's a search box".

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.770
The paper concludes that wage transparency,

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:39.229
that knowing whether you're keeping up with
the Joneses,

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:40.789
or the Johansens,

00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:43.240
doesn't make the populace happier overall.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.719
Instead, it increases the happiness gap:

00:01:46.719 --> 00:01:48.950
knowing for certain you're being paid more
than your neighbours

00:01:48.950 --> 00:01:52.049
makes you more likely to be satisfied with
your life.

00:01:52.049 --> 00:01:55.840
Knowing for certain that you're not doing
as well? That hurts.

00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:58.899
You can still look up people's tax returns
online in Norway, by the way.

00:01:58.899 --> 00:02:03.090
But lately, you have to log in as a taxpayer
yourself,

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:06.369
and everyone can see the records of who's
been searching for them.

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:09.269
That's a whole different social game right
there.

00:02:09.269 --> 00:02:13.150
But there is another good argument for wage
transparency:

00:02:13.150 --> 00:02:15.360
that it makes for a fairer world.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:17.659
There's no strong data on that yet, not that
I can find,

00:02:17.659 --> 00:02:20.300
and it'd be extremely difficult to measure.

00:02:20.300 --> 00:02:22.230
But there's some good anecdotal evidence,

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.989
particularly from an engineer at Google who
created a spreadsheet

00:02:24.989 --> 00:02:28.390
where their colleagues could voluntarily share
their salaries.

00:02:28.390 --> 00:02:33.190
The story around it, and how management reacted
to that, is well worth a read.

00:02:33.190 --> 00:02:34.970
And there's also evidence that,

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:37.170
although politicians might not like it,

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:41.780
public disclosure of tax returns does reduce
tax evasion.

00:02:41.780 --> 00:02:44.060
So does this mean I'm about to share my tax
return,

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:47.209
and report to the world how much I made last
year?

00:02:47.209 --> 00:02:49.840
No. Because I'm British,

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.750
because we don't talk about money,

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:54.450
you might find out something you don't want
to know...

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:57.269
or you might hurt someone else.

00:02:57.269 --> 00:03:02.310
So my reaction to wage transparency is the
same as a lot of people's:

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:03.410
you first.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.960
